{"count": 1, "errors": [], "gaps": [], "body": {"words": 0, "sections": []}, "lenses": [{"code": "single", "name": "One statement", "section": null, "instruction": "Does the body state exactly one thing? An \"and\", a bulleted list of behaviours, or\ntwo sentences each with their own verb mean this is two requirements. Split it \u2014\ntwo records each with their own `realized_in` are worth more than one that cannot\nbe satisfied or violated as a unit."}, {"code": "testable", "name": "Observable from outside", "section": null, "instruction": "Could someone who did not write this tell whether the system satisfies it, without\nreading the implementation? If the only way to check is to open the code, this is\ndescribing a design, not stating a requirement."}, {"code": "violation", "name": "What violating it looks like", "section": null, "instruction": "A constraint is a rule the system must never break, so the useful question is what\nbreaking it would look like from outside. If that cannot be described, this is\nprobably a preference \u2014 and a preference belongs in an `adr`, with its reasoning."}, {"code": "realized", "name": "Where it is satisfied", "section": null, "instruction": "`realized_in` is the only edge tying a rule to the code that satisfies it, and a\nrequirement without one is unfinished by design. If no component satisfies it yet,\nthat is the honest answer and the gap is the point \u2014 but check that it is not\nsimply unlinked."}]}
